(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-15"] = {"8.1":{"s":"A Bernoulli trial is a single trial with exactly two outcomes, called success and failure, in which the chance of success p stays the same every time and the trials do not affect one another. Tossing a coin, or whether a thrown die shows an even number, are examples. The chance of failure is q = 1 − p.","f":[{"x":"q = 1 - p","n":"Probability of failure, when p is the probability of success.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.245"}],"w":["Drawing without replacement is NOT a Bernoulli sequence: p changes after each draw."],"x":["A Bernoulli trial is a single trial of an experiment that has exactly two outcomes, called success and failure. The chance of success is p, and the chance of failure is q = 1 − p. Examples: tossing a coin (head is a success), throwing a die (an even number is a success), checking an item (it is defective or not).","When we repeat a Bernoulli trial, two conditions must hold. The trials must be independent, which means that the result of one does not change the others. And the chance of success p must be the same in every trial.","Drawing cards or balls without replacement breaks both conditions, since after each draw the numbers in the pack change. The same draws with replacement do form Bernoulli trials. For a large population, drawing a small sample without replacement is nearly Bernoulli.","Common mistake: using p and q for a situation in which p changes. Always ask: does the first result change the chance for the next one?"],"e":"A bag has 2 red and 3 blue balls. Drawing a ball and putting it back, a red ball is a success with p = 2/5 each time. Drawing without putting it back, the chance changes after each draw, so it is not a Bernoulli sequence."},"8.2":{"s":"In n independent Bernoulli trials, the number of successes X follows a binomial distribution with parameters n and p, written X ~ B(n, p). The probability of exactly x successes is the number of ways to choose which trials succeed, times p to the power x, times q to the power n − x. These probabilities are the terms of the binomial expansion of (q + p)ⁿ. The number of trials must be fixed and the trials independent.","f":[{"x":"P(X = x) = nC_x*q^(n - x)*p^x","n":"Binomial probability function, x = 0, 1, ..., n; q = 1 - p.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.248"},{"x":"P(X >= 1) = 1 - P(X = 0)","n":"'At least one': subtract the chance of none from 1 (the book's example on p.250 uses this).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.250"}],"w":["For 'at least one' use the complement: 1 − qⁿ is quicker than adding many terms."],"x":["Suppose a Bernoulli trial is repeated n times, independently, with the same chance of success p. Let X be the number of successes. Then X has a binomial distribution with the parameters n and p, written X ~ B(n, p). It can take the values 0, 1, 2, …, n.","To get exactly x successes, choose which x trials are the successes, in ⁿCₓ ways. Each such sequence has probability pˣ qⁿ⁻ˣ. So P(X = x) = ⁿCₓ qⁿ⁻ˣ pˣ. These are the terms of the expansion of (q + p)ⁿ, and since q + p = 1, they add up to 1.","Check the conditions before using it: a fixed number of trials, two outcomes, independent trials, and a constant p. For example, the number of sixes in 10 throws of a die is binomial. But the number of throws until the first six is not, because the number of trials is not fixed.","For a probability like at least one success, use the complement: P(X ≥ 1) = 1 − P(X = 0) = 1 − qⁿ. It is much quicker than adding n terms. Common mistake: mixing up p and q in the formula, or using permutations in place of combinations."],"e":"A die is thrown 4 times. The chance of exactly two sixes: X ~ B(4, 1/6), P(X = 2) = ⁴C₂ (1/6)² (5/6)² = 6 × 25/1296 = 0.1157. The chance of at least one six is 1 − (5/6)⁴ = 0.5177."},"8.3":{"s":"For a binomial distribution B(n, p) the mean (expected value) is np and the variance is npq, where q = 1 − p. The standard deviation is the square root of the variance. These formulas save finding every probability when only the average and the spread are needed.","f":[{"x":"E(X) = mu = n*p","n":"Mean of the binomial distribution B(n, p).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.250"},{"x":"Var(X) = n*p*q","n":"Variance; q = 1 - p.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.250"},{"x":"SD(X) = sqrt(Var(X)) = sqrt(n*p*q)","n":"Standard deviation.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.250"}],"w":["The variance npq is always less than the mean np, because q < 1."],"x":["For a binomial distribution B(n, p), there are simple formulas for the average and the spread, and you do not need to find every probability. The mean is E(X) = np. The variance is Var(X) = npq. The standard deviation is the square root of the variance, √(npq).","The reason for the mean: on average, a fraction p of the n trials succeed. In 100 tosses of a fair coin, you expect 50 heads. The variance is npq. It is largest when p = 1/2, and it gets smaller when p is near 0 or 1, because then the outcome is almost sure.","Since q is less than 1, the variance npq is always less than the mean np. This gives a quick check. If a problem gives a variance larger than the mean, it cannot be a binomial distribution. If you are given the mean and the variance, you can find q = variance/mean, then p = 1 − q, and n = mean/p.","Both the mean and the variance increase when n increases. Common mistake: giving √(npq) as the variance, or forgetting to take the square root when the standard deviation is asked."],"e":"X ~ B(24, 1/4). The mean is np = 24 × 1/4 = 6. The variance is npq = 6 × 3/4 = 4.5. The standard deviation is √4.5 = 2.12. Given a mean 6 and a variance 4.5, q = 0.75, p = 0.25 and n = 24."}};
